Forget the global last context in eglDestroyContext

Gesher issue #485 (Moovit: MapLibre GL maps in several GLSurfaceViews, each
with its own GL thread, on the one default display): "host fault reading 0x8"
at libGLESv2.dll+0x1ebea2, rx::Framebuffer11::markAttachmentsDirty with
this == NULL, called from rx::StateManager11::updateState during a draw.

Gesher builds ANGLE with angle_force_context_check_every_call
(compat/angle/args.gn). Each GL entry point then calls
DirtyContextIfNeeded(context) (src/libGLESv2/global_state.h), which marks all
state of the calling context dirty only when it differs from the global last
context. The Direct3D 11 back end has one StateManager11 per display, shared by
all contexts; StateManager11::onMakeCurrent resets its caches of the draw
framebuffer, executable and vertex array (mFramebuffer11, mExecutableD3D,
mVertexArray11) to null, and only the context being made current is dirtied.

egl::Display::destroyContext (src/libANGLE/Display.cpp) makes the doomed
context current with Display::makeCurrent and then restores the thread's
previous context, bypassing SetContextCurrent, so the global last context is
not updated. The ScopedSyncCurrentContextFromThread in the DestroyContext stub
then calls SetContextCurrent with the thread's context; when that is null,
DirtyContextIfNeeded does nothing. Sequence:

  thread B: eglMakeCurrent(EGL_NO_CONTEXT) releasing context X
  thread A: a GL call (last context = A; glClear applies only the clear
            state, the draw framebuffer binding among it)
  thread B: eglDestroyContext(X) (the StateManager11 caches become null)
  thread A: draw: A is the last context, so it is not dirtied again; the
            executable and vertex array, still pending from the dirtying at
            the clear, are re-applied, the draw framebuffer is not, and
            updateState dereferences the null mFramebuffer11.

GLSurfaceView's GL thread ends exactly like thread B (eglMakeCurrent with
EGL_NO_CONTEXT, eglDestroySurface, eglDestroyContext). When the destroying
thread has another context current, restoring it dirties that context and
records it as the last context, so the other contexts are dirtied again and
nothing breaks. eglMakeCurrent and eglReleaseThread run onMakeCurrent only for
the context that they make current, which SetContextCurrent records;
eglTerminate releases contexts without making them current.

The fix: the DestroyContext stub sets the global last context to null before
egl::Display::destroyContext. Every context, including one that made the
previous GL call on another thread, then has all of its state dirtied before
its next GL call; the context current on the destroying thread, if any, is
dirtied by the scoped sync and recorded as the last context. The global EGL
mutex, which eglDestroyContext's entry point holds, guards every access to the
last context. This also drops the last-context pointer to a context that may
be freed here.

Written against ANGLE ab6a82e0ed14, the commit Gesher builds. On ANGLE main
(November 2026) DirtyContextIfNeeded is unchanged but the DestroyContext stub
was refactored, so the hunk needs rebasing when ANGLE_COMMIT moves; not yet
reported upstream. The patch is under ANGLE's BSD license. Applied by compat/angle/build-angle.ps1; regression tests:
tests/native/egl_context_destroy_test.cc (track 40-native-libs) and
tests/native2/egl_context_destroy_jni.cpp (track 44-native2).

diff --git a/src/libGLESv2/egl_stubs.cpp b/src/libGLESv2/egl_stubs.cpp
--- a/src/libGLESv2/egl_stubs.cpp
+++ b/src/libGLESv2/egl_stubs.cpp
@@ -43,6 +43,26 @@
     }
     *numConfigs = resultSize;
 }
+
+// Gesher (compat/angle/patches): with ANGLE_FORCE_CONTEXT_CHECK_EVERY_CALL a GL call dirties all
+// state of its context only when that context is not the global last context. eglDestroyContext
+// makes the doomed context current inside egl::Display::destroyContext and then restores the
+// thread's previous context (or none), without SetContextCurrent. Making the doomed context
+// current runs the backend's onMakeCurrent, which resets per-display state that all contexts share
+// (the D3D11 StateManager11 caches of the draw framebuffer, executable and vertex array). If the
+// destroying thread has no current context, nothing updates the last context, so the context that
+// made the previous GL call, possibly on another thread, would draw with the reset caches.
+// Forget the last context so that every context has all of its state dirtied before its next
+// call (this also drops the pointer to a context that may be freed here). eglMakeCurrent and
+// eglReleaseThread need no such step: they run onMakeCurrent only for the context that they make
+// current, which SetContextCurrent then records. Called with the global EGL mutex held, which
+// guards every access to the last context.
+void InvalidateGlobalLastContext()
+{
+#if defined(ANGLE_FORCE_CONTEXT_CHECK_EVERY_CALL)
+    SetGlobalLastContext(nullptr);
+#endif
+}
 }  // anonymous namespace
 
 EGLBoolean BindAPI(Thread *thread, EGLenum api)
@@ -313,6 +333,7 @@
 
     ScopedSyncCurrentContextFromThread scopedSyncCurrent(thread);
 
+    InvalidateGlobalLastContext();
     ANGLE_EGL_TRY_RETURN(thread, display->destroyContext(thread, context), "eglDestroyContext",
                          GetContextIfValid(display, contextID), EGL_FALSE);
     thread->setSuccess();
